 1 3.802-3.12Zm-3.802 8.456h2.888c.178 1.23.484 2.335.914 3.12a6.023 6.023 0 0 1-3.802-3.12Zm6.938 3.12c.43-.785.735-1.89.914-3.12h2.887a6.023 6.023 0 0 1-3.801 3.12Z"></path></svg>Visit Website</a></li></ul></div></div><div><div class="duet--article--map-card hkfm3h0" data-slug="emmy-lous" id="emmy-lous"><div class="hkfm3h2"></div><div class="hkfm3h3"><h2 class="hkfm3h5">Emmy Lou’s</h2><span class="hkfm3h6"><button type="button" class="_6ylzph0" aria-label="Save to a list"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="2 2 20 20" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7.2002 20.3202L12.4176 15.0407L17.6349 20.3202V8.24064C17.6349 5.67699 15.43 3.6001 12.7113 3.6001H7.2002V20.3202Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7"></path></svg></button></span></div><p class="duet--article--dangerously-set-cms-markup duet--article--standard-paragraph u67w0ti va4xi81 va4xi80 e8cira1 hkfm3h9">Open since December, this adorable, classy wine bar and bottle shop bucks the stereotype of bad wine bar food. A surprisingly delicious small plates menu dares to outshine the wines while pleasing the palate: try assorted meat and cheese plates, exquisite hamachi tiradito ($16), wagyu carpaccio that is art on a plate ($18), or pasta salad with lots of fluffy parmesan ($12). Don’t forget dessert: at the time of writing, it’s the most inventive sundae in town, with zesty orange and ginger ice cream, carrot caramel, and topped with pistachio. Lunchtime offers rotating sandwiches. Wines by-the-glass and take-home bottles are priced from $12 and $25, respectively, and showcase bottles from around the world.</p><div class="iyvbja0"><button type="button" class="iyvbja1" aria-pressed="false"><svg width="12" height="10" viewBox="0 0 12 10" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path fill="currentColor" d="M11.2226 0.779264C10.1836 -0.259755 8.49832 -0.259755 7.4593 0.779264L6.00095 2.23761L4.5426 0.779264C3.50359 -0.259755 1.81828 